/*! \page Solver_build Building the solver

The solver needs a C++17 compiler, CMake 3.15 or newer, and the \ref sundials library.

\verbatim
cd radcluster_code
cmake -S cpp_utils -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build --config Release
\endverbatim

The binary is written to build, or to build/Release with multi-configuration generators. The bridge cpp_bridge.py looks for it in both places. The driver notebook builds the solver on first use, and builds it again when the platform changes.

\section Solver_build_optional Optional dependencies

Each optional dependency is detected at configure time.

| Dependency | Enables | If missing |
|---|---|---|
| LAPACK/BLAS | the \ref Solver_preconditioner | it is compiled out, and the solver falls back to the Jacobi preconditioner at run time |
| OpenMP | the parallel right-hand side of the active-window mode | that mode runs on one thread |
| SuiteSparse/KLU, with SUNDIALS built with -DENABLE_KLU=ON | the sparse-direct linear solver | that option stops at run time with a diagnostic |

\section Solver_build_flags Compiler flags

The optimization flags are -O3 -march=native, or /arch:AVX2 /GL /fp:precise with Microsoft Visual C++.

\b Note that fast-math is deliberately not enabled. It alters IEEE-754 semantics, and it can make the error estimator of CVODE diverge on these stiff systems.

On Windows the build copies the required run-time libraries next to the executable.

\sa \ref container, \ref hoffman2

*/
